\title{metabeta: amortized Bayesian mixed models with a 'brms' interface}
\description{
\code{metabeta} provides the user interface of \pkg{brms} on top of the pretrained
amortized posterior estimator of the \code{metabeta} Python package. Replace
\code{library(brms)} with \code{library(metabeta)}, and calls such as \code{brm()},
\code{summary()}, \code{fixef()}, \code{ranef()}, \code{posterior_predict()}, \code{loo()} or
\code{hypothesis()} keep working and return objects of the same shape.
}
\section{Supported models}{

\itemize{
\item Likelihoods: gaussian (identity link), bernoulli or binomial with one trial
(logit link), and poisson (log link).
\item Population-level effects: anything \code{\link[stats:model.matrix]{stats::model.matrix()}} can build, with
up to 16 coefficients including the intercept.
\item Group-level effects on one grouping factor (possibly an interaction such
as \code{g1:g2}), either all correlated or all uncorrelated. The number q of
group-level coefficients is limited by the number d of population-level
coefficients: q <= 2, 3, 4, 5 for d <= 4, 8, 12, 16.
\item Data: 5 to 200 groups with 5 to 150 observations each, at most 3000 in total.
\item Priors: \code{normal()} or \code{student_t(5, ...)} on population-level effects (one
family for all); \code{normal(0, ...)}, \code{student_t(5, 0, ...)} or
\code{exponential()} on standard deviations and \code{sigma}; \code{lkj()} on
correlations. Default priors are weakly informative instead of flat.
}

Other models raise an error explaining why; fit them with \pkg{brms}.
}

\section{How the posterior is computed}{

The design is built with \code{\link[stats:model.matrix]{stats::model.matrix()}} and mapped linearly onto the
standardized scale the network was trained on. Priors are mapped to that
scale and draws back from it exactly, so parameters mean what they mean in
\pkg{brms}. One exception: with correlated group-level slopes, the network
needs centered predictors, so the priors on \code{sd(Intercept)} and the
correlations apply to the group-level intercept at the predictor means. The
reported draws are still those of the intercept at zero.

With \code{algorithm = "sampling"} (the default), the network's posterior is the
proposal of independence Metropolis-Hastings chains that target the exact
posterior, so \code{Rhat} and the effective sample sizes are genuine MCMC
diagnostics. A low acceptance rate is reported as a warning.
}

\section{Differences to brms}{

\itemize{
\item Stan tuning arguments (\code{cores}, \code{backend}, \code{init}, entries of \code{control}
other than \code{random_walk}, ...) are ignored. Functions that need Stan, and brms functions for features metabeta
cannot fit, raise an error that says so.
\item The draws contain \code{lprior} but not \code{lp__}.
\item Fitted models have class \code{c("metabetafit", "brmsfit")}, so packages that use
generic functions (\pkg{posterior}, \pkg{bayesplot}, \pkg{loo},
\pkg{tidybayes}, ...) accept them. Load metabeta instead of \pkg{brms}, not
alongside it.
}
}

